1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,519
Architecture interviews do not reward
buzzword bingo. They reward trade-off
stories under constraints. Someone asks
you to design an order system.

2
00:00:09,639 --> 00:00:16,239
A weak answer sprays Kafka, Kubernetes,
CQRS, and mesh until the whiteboard fills.

3
00:00:16,359 --> 00:00:29,640
A strong answer clarifies requirements and
SLOs first — write throughput, read
latency, consistency needs, team size,
cost ceiling — then draws boundaries and
data flow that fit those constraints.

4
00:00:29,759 --> 00:00:40,119
Look at this text for a moment. Walk that
scaffold as speech. Requirements and SLOs:
what must never be lost, what may be
eventually consistent, what percentile
matters.

5
00:00:40,239 --> 00:00:52,279
Ask questions — interviewers reward
candidates who surface constraints instead
of inventing infinity. API and data:
resources, ownership, sync versus async
edges.

6
00:00:52,399 --> 00:01:05,319
Runtime as a Java engineer: JVM heap
sizing, GC pause budgets, thread models,
cold start if scale-to-zero. Failure
modes: dependency down, duplicate
messages, partial deploy.

7
00:01:05,439 --> 00:01:14,799
Observability: metrics, traces, alerts on
SLOs. Evolve complexity: start modular,
extract a service when a boundary earns
independence.

8
00:01:14,920 --> 00:01:27,840
Stage deliberately — single region before
multi-region, monolith modules before
services, cache before CQRS — and name the
pain each step removes. Practice a short
design aloud.

9
00:01:27,960 --> 00:01:42,280
"Design a URL shortener" clarifies
read/write ratio, redirect latency SLO,
consistency on create, and analytics
needs. Maybe a modular monolith is enough
at small scale. Maybe reads need caching
with TTL.

10
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:58,160
Maybe writes append events for analytics.
JVM notes: heap for cache, GC pause budget
versus redirect SLO, connection pools to
the store. Failure modes: cache stampede,
DB outage, duplicate short-code
generation.

11
00:01:58,280 --> 00:02:09,120
Observability: redirect latency histogram,
error rate, dependency health. That answer
is coherent without naming twelve
products. Draw boundaries with words if
you lack a whiteboard.

12
00:02:09,240 --> 00:02:21,640
"Clients hit an API gateway; the orders
service owns the orders database;
inventory is a separate service reached
synchronously for reservation and
asynchronously for restock events." Then
pressure-test:

13
00:02:21,760 --> 00:02:27,600
gateway timeout policy, reservation
idempotency, event outbox, read-your-
writes needs.

14
00:02:27,720 --> 00:02:35,280
Each pressure-test adds a box or arrow you
missed. Architecture is iterative speech,
not a single perfect diagram.

15
00:02:35,400 --> 00:02:47,280
Bring JVM awareness as a differentiator:
you know that "just add instances" still
meets GC, metaspace, and thread limits per
process. If you propose caching, mention
invalidation.

16
00:02:47,400 --> 00:02:59,920
If you propose events, mention at-least-
once and idempotency. Ignoring ops — how
it deploys, migrates, rolls back, and
pages — is how architecture answers sound
academic.

17
00:03:00,040 --> 00:03:08,880
A design that needs a two-hour migration
with no expand/contract plan is not a
senior design. Tie this wrap to the JVM
wrap from Episode Sixty-Six.

18
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:16,000
Same scaffold spirit: define, mechanism,
failure mode, diagnose — now scaled to
systems.

19
00:03:16,120 --> 00:03:25,560
For "why not microservices here?" define
the trade-off, explain network failure
modes, name the small-team failure mode of
premature distribution,

20
00:03:25,680 --> 00:03:30,080
and say how you would revisit with metrics
on deploy friction and scaling pain.

21
00:03:30,200 --> 00:03:41,640
Buzzword bingo fails because follow-ups
expose missing mechanisms. Trade-off
stories survive follow-ups because they
already admitted what hurts. How to answer
as a Java engineer?

22
00:03:41,820 --> 00:03:53,380
Requirements, design, JVM and runtime
concerns, failure modes, observability —
practice until the order is muscle memory,
then let the specific domain fill the
blanks.

23
00:03:53,500 --> 00:04:01,660
Admit trade-offs: microservices versus
modular monolith, consistency versus
availability, freshness versus cache hit
rate.

24
00:04:01,780 --> 00:04:15,540
This wrap sets you up for deeper
production topics that still show up in
senior conversations: caching, API
contracts, events, performance loops, and
readiness. Caching comes first — latency
bought with correctness risk.
